1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,520
This lesson maps every one of the eleven current Week Two Moodle
links: eight video demonstrations and three EMSWorld articles. It
teaches their central ideas in new words; it is not a subtitle dump,
video replacement or official school presentation.

2
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:32,208
The first four demonstrations show i-gel placement, a proposed suction
add-on, a King LTS-D device and rigid-tip pharyngeal suction. Compare
each with the current BCEHS procedure and the actual skill your
instructor assigns.

3
00:00:32,208 --> 00:00:48,096
The intubation and Melker videos show advanced procedures; the CPAP
videos show a Queensland service and a specific Rescuer 8700 product.
They explain what those sources demonstrate, not what every BC PCP may
perform.

4
00:00:48,096 --> 00:01:04,152
The final three articles revisit acute PE, COPD exacerbation and
undifferentiated breathlessness. Use them to practise clinical
reasoning, then cross-check any threshold, intervention or scope
statement against the current local guideline.

5
00:01:04,152 --> 00:01:16,032
A video can be technically clear yet still use a different device,
country, protocol revision or provider licence. Accurate observation
is not the same as local authorization.

6
00:01:16,032 --> 00:01:29,664
Ask who produced the demonstration, what device model is shown, and
whether it is a service training video, manufacturer marketing, or a
general skills clip. Those categories carry different evidentiary
weight.

7
00:01:29,664 --> 00:01:45,168
For BC PCP field practice, the current BCEHS handbook, applicable PCP
procedure, actual school station and your instructor’s sign-off
govern. For a device-specific detail, use the exact approved device
instructions as well.

8
00:01:45,168 --> 00:02:02,016
For airway support, the endpoint is an effective, reassessed breath:
appropriate chest rise, airflow, patient response and available
monitoring. A tube or mask that is merely in place is not proof of
effective oxygenation or ventilation.

9
00:02:02,016 --> 00:02:15,168
The BCEHS Professional Practice i-gel video demonstrates preparation,
insertion, fixation and troubleshooting. Its useful learning point is
that placement has to be followed by a real ventilation check.

10
00:02:15,168 --> 00:02:29,880
Current PR08 requires prior ability to ventilate with a bag-valve mask
and lists airway obstruction, active vomiting and other exclusions.
The SGA is a staged option, not a shortcut around a blocked airway.

11
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:42,936
The current procedure describes the i-gel rather than the separate
King device shown in another video. Confirm the correct school-
supplied size and model; do not mix manufacturers’ parts, cuff
assumptions or holders.

12
00:02:42,936 --> 00:02:56,664
If placement fails, return to effective BVM with the required filter
and seek help. If placement appears successful, continue to reassess
after every movement or change in patient condition.

13
00:02:56,664 --> 00:03:13,248
The BCEHS suction video describes a nasogastric tube and historic
pandemic-era equipment. Do not reproduce that technique from the
video: PCP pharyngeal suction is limited by current PR27 and does not
authorize gastric intubation.

14
00:03:13,248 --> 00:03:28,656
PR27 specifies the supplied Resus Pack catheter, a package-based depth
measurement with a two-centimetre allowance, lubricant and a clear
depth mark. The device-specific step must be learned on the actual
supervised trainer.

15
00:03:28,656 --> 00:03:48,096
The procedure uses 80 mmHg and says not generally to exceed 160 mmHg.
Stop when fluid clears or after 15 to 20 seconds without fluid;
continuous suction may harm. These limits belong to this procedure,
not every suction task.

16
00:03:48,096 --> 00:04:04,512
Current PR08 and PR27 require removal of the i-gel during active
vomiting or difficult ventilation after vomiting, followed by
oropharyngeal clearance and restoration of effective support. Never
let the old video override this branch.

17
00:04:04,512 --> 00:04:19,584
The WCTC Fire and EMS skills video presents a rigid-tip oral suction
example for vomit, blood and secretions. It emphasizes maintaining
sight of the tip and recognizing that a rigid catheter can cause
trauma.

18
00:04:19,584 --> 00:04:33,096
Its advice about use in a moving ambulance belongs to that US
educational demonstration. Do not turn it into a BC service rule
without a current local source; instead learn the assigned school
safety standard.

19
00:04:33,096 --> 00:04:46,800
Suction is effective only if the airway is cleared and ventilation
resumes. The screen’s depiction of suction, the sound of the pump or
an empty canister does not establish that the patient is now breathing
adequately.

20
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:59,976
The technique, suction level, insertion limits and infection-control
setup must match the exact school equipment and current procedure.
Rehearse on a trainer with the instructor watching and correcting your
hand position.

21
00:04:59,976 --> 00:05:13,296
The Live Action Safety overview is a King Systems product
demonstration of the LTS-D, including cuffs, ventilation openings, an
alternate suction or gastric-access lumen, and device-specific
positioning.

22
00:05:13,296 --> 00:05:27,696
The video’s cuff volumes, pressure advice, size ranges and gastric-
tube claims belong to that manufacturer’s device and training context.
They are not transferable instructions for i-gel or the school’s PCP
airway station.

23
00:05:27,696 --> 00:05:44,616
Current BCEHS PR08 names the i-gel as the local supraglottic airway
procedure and sets conditions, contraindications, prior BVM
confirmation and securement. A general resemblance between devices
does not expand the approved equipment list.

24
00:05:44,616 --> 00:05:59,304
For the exam, be able to state what a supraglottic device aims to
accomplish and why its seal is not equivalent to a cuffed tracheal
tube. For hands-on practice, use only the device and sequence
specified by the instructor.

25
00:05:59,304 --> 00:06:15,552
The Moodle item named “Endotracheal Intubation” links to a clip titled
“Endotracheal Ventilation.” It shows laryngoscopy, tube placement and
auscultation in a general training context; it does not establish a BC
PCP procedure.

26
00:06:15,552 --> 00:06:30,552
The Melker video is a product-specific emergency cricothyrotomy
demonstration using a wire and dilator catheter set. Current BCEHS
PR22 instead defines its own age-specific techniques and provider
scope.

27
00:06:30,552 --> 00:06:47,472
BCEHS B01 places intubation within the ACP pathway, and PR22 applies
to ACP and higher. These videos are useful for recognizing what
advanced escalation involves, not for unsupervised rehearsal or
claiming PCP authorization.

28
00:06:47,472 --> 00:07:04,104
Your PCP learning task is to recognize deteriorating oxygenation or
ventilation, continue authorized basic support, summon appropriate
resources early and communicate the patient’s response. Do not delay
BVM while discussing an advanced procedure.

29
00:07:04,104 --> 00:07:19,056
The Queensland Ambulance Service clip introduces a disposable CPAP
design and explains how oxygen flow through a valve creates continuous
pressure. That helps visualize the mechanism, not the current BC
service protocol.

30
00:07:19,056 --> 00:07:33,840
The video was made for an Australian service rollout in 2013. Its
device, flow rates, pressure escalation, eligibility and documentation
cannot be transplanted into the BCEHS procedure or a different mask.

31
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:50,952
CPAP provides continuous positive pressure while the patient breathes;
it does not squeeze a breath into the lungs. If respiratory drive,
tidal volume or consciousness is failing, the patient needs an
authorized ventilation pathway, not CPAP as a substitute.

32
00:07:50,952 --> 00:08:05,952
Use the mechanism to understand why a mask leak, intolerance,
vomiting, low pressure or deterioration matters. Then apply the
current BCEHS PR09 criteria, contraindications and reassessment
instructions.

33
00:08:05,952 --> 00:08:20,832
The Rescuer 8700 demonstration shows a silicone mask, adjustable
headgear, pressure control, a medication port and an anti-suffocation
valve. These are product features, not universal CPAP components.

34
00:08:20,832 --> 00:08:33,912
Its instructions about flow, pressure adjustment and medication
delivery apply to that product and its own instructions for use. Do
not infer the school’s current device or treatment settings from a
product video title.

35
00:08:33,912 --> 00:08:49,368
The anti-suffocation valve is a device safeguard; it does not correct
respiratory arrest, hypoventilation, reduced consciousness or
aspiration risk. The person still requires continuous assessment and a
backup plan.

36
00:08:49,368 --> 00:09:03,552
Treat all pressure and interface changes as device-specific. In class,
inspect the exact approved system with your instructor and state the
current PR09 patient criteria before rehearsing its application.

37
00:09:03,552 --> 00:09:21,240
Current BCEHS PR09 applies to PCPs with AIME CPAP training and higher;
prior CliniCall consultation is recommended for PCPs. The patient must
be at least thirteen, awake, able to follow commands and able to
maintain an open airway.

38
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:38,304
The listed respiratory-distress pattern includes respiratory rate
above twenty-four per minute, oxygen saturation below ninety-four
percent on supplemental oxygen, and accessory-muscle use. Apply all
current criteria together rather than choosing one number.

39
00:09:38,304 --> 00:09:58,848
PR09 excludes age twelve and under, reduced consciousness or inability
to follow commands, respiratory arrest or hypoventilation, inability
to fit the mask, vomiting or aspiration risk, traumatic distress,
tracheostomy, suspected pneumothorax and systolic pressure below
ninety.

40
00:09:58,848 --> 00:10:17,400
Explain the treatment and obtain consent; position and monitor as
directed; fit and reassess the mask; follow the current device chart
and PR09 ceilings. If the patient worsens, stop treating the device as
the goal and move to the authorized backup pathway.

41
00:10:17,400 --> 00:10:34,056
The school-linked 2015 EMSWorld article uses a postoperative trauma
case to teach why recent surgery, injury and reduced mobility matter.
Its population statistics and US-specific treatment statements are
historical, not current BC instructions.

42
00:10:34,056 --> 00:10:47,784
A pulmonary embolus obstructs pulmonary blood flow. The resulting
dead-space and gas-exchange problem can combine with increased
pulmonary vascular resistance and right-ventricular strain, reducing
effective forward flow.

43
00:10:47,784 --> 00:11:07,416
Ask about recent surgery, hospitalization, reduced mobility, prior
venous thromboembolism, malignancy, pregnancy or hormonal change.
Sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, cough, syncope or hypotension can
raise concern, but no one finding proves PE.

44
00:11:07,416 --> 00:11:26,568
Current BCEHS B06 calls for supportive care, oxygen as required,
monitoring and rapid conveyance; unstable patients need a transport
plan that preserves resuscitation capability. Do not use an old
article’s scoring table or fluid/drug plan as a PCP order.

45
00:11:26,568 --> 00:11:41,544
The 2015 EMSWorld COPD article illustrates a useful diagnostic habit:
compare with the patient’s usual function and include competing causes
such as heart failure, pulmonary embolism, pneumonia and pneumothorax.

46
00:11:41,544 --> 00:11:55,680
An exacerbation is an acute change from baseline, often increased
breathlessness, more frequent or severe cough, or a change in sputum.
A familiar diagnosis does not rule out a second new emergency.

47
00:11:55,680 --> 00:12:13,680
Current BCEHS B05 describes COPD as primarily a ventilation problem
and warns that critical hypercarbia may occur even when the
respiratory rate looks high. Watch the trend: falling consciousness,
decreasing rate and shrinking tidal volumes are red flags.

48
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:32,232
B05 says to use the patient’s usual saturation when readily known,
titrate to the current 88-92 percent target, and never withhold oxygen
from an acutely short-of-breath patient. Avoid exertion during
movement, convey early and follow current medication scope.

49
00:12:32,232 --> 00:12:43,992
The 2009 Shortness of Breath article connects respiratory mechanics
with cardiac causes and demonstrates that dyspnea can occur with
myocardial ischemia even when chest pain is absent.

50
00:12:43,992 --> 00:12:59,136
The article also surveys COPD, asthma-like wheeze, infection and heart
failure, but its old statistics, labels and treatment examples should
not be memorized as current practice. Use current BCEHS pages for
actual clinical direction.

51
00:12:59,136 --> 00:13:13,224
Build a time course: abrupt or progressive, at rest or exertion,
positional, infection-linked or trauma-linked. Ask what is normal for
this person and what changed before selecting a cause.

52
00:13:13,224 --> 00:13:35,328
Observe work of breathing, speech, posture, mental status, skin, chest
movement and sounds. Trend pulse, respirations, blood pressure and
SpO2; use ECG or capnography when indicated and within your training.
A treatment response can guide reassessment but does not prove a
diagnosis.

53
00:13:35,328 --> 00:13:53,208
An adult reports sudden shortness of breath six days after knee
surgery and several days of limited movement. The first observations
are respiratory rate thirty-two, pulse one hundred twenty-two, oxygen
saturation eighty-eight percent and systolic pressure eighty-six.

54
00:13:53,208 --> 00:14:07,176
The history makes pulmonary embolism important to consider, but the
observations do not prove it. Assess for other immediately dangerous
causes while recognizing that hypotension and sudden onset indicate
instability.

55
00:14:07,176 --> 00:14:25,656
Use the current patient assessment and B06 pathway: support airway,
oxygenation and ventilation as needed; obtain serial findings; arrange
rapid conveyance and additional resources. Plan movement so that
effective resuscitation remains possible.

56
00:14:25,656 --> 00:14:41,400
Do not delay conveyance to prove a diagnosis, apply an old score,
start a video’s historical treatment or perform a procedure outside
PCP scope. Give the receiving team the onset, risk factors, trends,
support and response.

57
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:56,208
First: the Queensland clip is a 2013 Australian service demonstration
of a different device. Use only the current PR09 and the exact
approved product instructions; continuous pressure does not replace a
breath.

58
00:14:56,208 --> 00:15:13,368
Second: PR27 describes a measured, marked, limited pharyngeal suction
method. It is not gastric intubation or continuous suction; active
vomiting calls for device removal, oropharyngeal clearance and
restoration of support.

59
00:15:13,368 --> 00:15:27,864
Third: decreasing consciousness, respiratory rate or tidal volume
suggests impending failure even if a previous saturation looked
acceptable. Use the patient and trend, not one isolated number.

60
00:15:27,864 --> 00:15:44,928
Fourth: recent surgery and immobility raise PE concern, but do not
confirm PE. Reassess broadly, support the unstable patient, follow B06
and arrange rapid conveyance without importing historical US article
treatment.
